[{:db/id "q-80643/selection/9d25691703fa4a2983e0900290503e52acda58fe5adc853faa3fcf6d3ce06adf" :answer/id #uuid "96256430-0f0d-5676-be98-04ebb029fed5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80643/selection/76f9a7b136339af9d403ec90bfb3ab728dd5ab89af703cb9c628893ebeafe535" :answer/id #uuid "40300916-9120-5584-96fa-d3ba36d9087c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80643/selection/c2da857b17937e74cca2d7f2d86a2b11a241429b313985e51f0e906f8d7e6b17" :answer/id #uuid "18b93d76-ef1c-59eb-b1a3-21868b78ae71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=1,\\,b=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80643/selection/a8f438e3d08e90b3bbbf5544e9c8bbb11e31545fde90a74e65a6fbae58c8888f" :answer/id #uuid "88b1c90f-5f48-5307-bf1e-c1dfcedf2534" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80643/selection/96e2a1e0db8992f8c06a79efb15b6edc2113608f41db0467163a4651a75f1b5b" :answer/id #uuid "8b91c500-26e4-55b0-96c9-fd6731a5f34e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80643/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "72770a9f-4146-5bcc-aabc-abe7420a652e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80643/selection/76f9a7b136339af9d403ec90bfb3ab728dd5ab89af703cb9c628893ebeafe535" :answer-field/choices ["q-80643/selection/9d25691703fa4a2983e0900290503e52acda58fe5adc853faa3fcf6d3ce06adf" "q-80643/selection/76f9a7b136339af9d403ec90bfb3ab728dd5ab89af703cb9c628893ebeafe535" "q-80643/selection/c2da857b17937e74cca2d7f2d86a2b11a241429b313985e51f0e906f8d7e6b17" "q-80643/selection/a8f438e3d08e90b3bbbf5544e9c8bbb11e31545fde90a74e65a6fbae58c8888f" "q-80643/selection/96e2a1e0db8992f8c06a79efb15b6edc2113608f41db0467163a4651a75f1b5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80643" :question/id #uuid "ffd78bce-479a-57bc-84cf-9932c814309e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80643" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2, \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x) \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a{x}^{2}y+6x=3b{x}^{2}y+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}y$, we see that $a=\\frac{3b}{2}.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{y}(-1,1)=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3 \\\\ b(-1)^{3}(1)+3(-1)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ -b+3+2 & =3 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na=\\frac{3b}{2}=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133221/selection/d5c00477be4fdf4fc017384c26d3a5670fe23a1ebfe549b0a9319a67ea0cd3e1" :answer/id #uuid "da47f82d-bfe4-5b61-980b-5e300f5fc698" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133221/selection/5914b4561ed0c79d708722bde9025694668a44ed6a2da8ea7e2a60e44874426e" :answer/id #uuid "d1715457-c5bd-515f-93ed-a4940bbc67a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=5,\\,b=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133221/selection/b8b30d0e91d0660c55b332c49ffb1429297116272cd755b94a256851baaf7ce3" :answer/id #uuid "4c4f9f91-2059-51ad-b83c-1692b7fd76c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=6,\\,b=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133221/selection/607bb6fcc91a7fdc6bda8a00a61b6820789dc75ff2cfe2ef843729d4e2fcdb71" :answer/id #uuid "2abbda83-d1b3-5f14-9940-7bfc4b624ec0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133221/selection/75ed455905abefc3334b62c174695988ee17b051c638f6717c484f3a04ba6291" :answer/id #uuid "cbf1cf60-e038-5d2d-9dd1-03b55f43a97c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133221/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ca200be-837d-52f4-bd44-1d4f38f751d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-133221/selection/75ed455905abefc3334b62c174695988ee17b051c638f6717c484f3a04ba6291" :answer-field/choices ["q-133221/selection/d5c00477be4fdf4fc017384c26d3a5670fe23a1ebfe549b0a9319a67ea0cd3e1" "q-133221/selection/5914b4561ed0c79d708722bde9025694668a44ed6a2da8ea7e2a60e44874426e" "q-133221/selection/b8b30d0e91d0660c55b332c49ffb1429297116272cd755b94a256851baaf7ce3" "q-133221/selection/607bb6fcc91a7fdc6bda8a00a61b6820789dc75ff2cfe2ef843729d4e2fcdb71" "q-133221/selection/75ed455905abefc3334b62c174695988ee17b051c638f6717c484f3a04ba6291"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133221" :question/id #uuid "c7b780a7-788b-5d60-b089-16f56470436d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133221" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=axy-5{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-bxy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(axy-5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =ax-10y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-bxy) \\\\  & =2x-by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-10y=2x-by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=2$ and $b=10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133221/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65136"] :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}+bxy-8.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}-8y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+bxy-8) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}-8y=6{x}^{2}+by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=6$ and $b=-8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-80766/selection/9ff07697849911308b964118da37a9b62b1c10a02dcdd505e50901477b0c7ff4" :answer/id #uuid "ff85353a-52a8-5de5-a08c-c5f71469eae9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-e$, $b=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80766/selection/3512df352d5132ea525a3dcb193e180bbfc792411bb08d93f48e715006f87fc8" :answer/id #uuid "c0baf7c9-82b8-5751-9cec-0ea918fc08d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1$, $b=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80766/selection/f3fa9f58a1e5149c14fd6d5836df255bd574b018eae4de83be9f9611489352b2" :answer/id #uuid "f1ab101e-f70c-5d42-b4f2-c32f7702bf32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-2$, $b=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80766/selection/81331628f2807aa9846e590204215ed30bf29efbbe234bfa012ed9be072db998" :answer/id #uuid "3d11e34d-2427-5149-9188-400d18469543" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=e$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80766/selection/2e8afb1dca5c52d93084c7a946510ff8b0ee0aeea46cfe916c90fe390e34163e" :answer/id #uuid "d1e7383d-17b2-5d1b-bb88-bbd595e776a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1$, $b=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80766/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5dca220-71cd-5512-a684-16b086ee9f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80766/selection/f3fa9f58a1e5149c14fd6d5836df255bd574b018eae4de83be9f9611489352b2" :answer-field/choices ["q-80766/selection/9ff07697849911308b964118da37a9b62b1c10a02dcdd505e50901477b0c7ff4" "q-80766/selection/3512df352d5132ea525a3dcb193e180bbfc792411bb08d93f48e715006f87fc8" "q-80766/selection/f3fa9f58a1e5149c14fd6d5836df255bd574b018eae4de83be9f9611489352b2" "q-80766/selection/81331628f2807aa9846e590204215ed30bf29efbbe234bfa012ed9be072db998" "q-80766/selection/2e8afb1dca5c52d93084c7a946510ff8b0ee0aeea46cfe916c90fe390e34163e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80766" :question/id #uuid "f63374b4-82d5-5092-bcef-8ec87e149ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80766" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}=b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x}$ and $x{y}^{2},$ respectively, we see that $b=1$ and $a=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80766/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80766"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80647/selection/cf0e877a0fd4bfcc31c625fe9569739c1b39f62de7f3a41885c78159fd231f05" :answer/id #uuid "b7a50334-a5e2-55f8-8d6c-d6d3eafd71db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1$, $b=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80647/selection/01c87525ea99d15f50f4159b9ee8bd234d6122ea4759dd6b2e7b3ee6e9fdb268" :answer/id #uuid "2338eab1-7879-5aa0-844a-f9ae5ca48f4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=-4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80647/selection/0bde71d378b15f1002b3efdfb581c94e0360ff585b3579922f0fce68d521db3b" :answer/id #uuid "54d0e5c2-a740-5c7c-ab84-44ac40e4ba8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80647/selection/20b58190948428aa77a583e526c08be96b94338704c4b9e276148a0b0b4fb2b6" :answer/id #uuid "5fa21fed-af6a-5271-b73d-c7110db252e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80647/selection/bc719e54be2c8060523f8e94ac84842d109a14e9f2239a7e02f7df837b08c2b4" :answer/id #uuid "6357b2bc-815d-5836-a230-eea14bd1ea47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=4$, $b=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80647/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1f6ac57-51cf-509d-8565-1e772997a007" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80647/selection/0bde71d378b15f1002b3efdfb581c94e0360ff585b3579922f0fce68d521db3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-80647/selection/cf0e877a0fd4bfcc31c625fe9569739c1b39f62de7f3a41885c78159fd231f05" "q-80647/selection/01c87525ea99d15f50f4159b9ee8bd234d6122ea4759dd6b2e7b3ee6e9fdb268" "q-80647/selection/0bde71d378b15f1002b3efdfb581c94e0360ff585b3579922f0fce68d521db3b" "q-80647/selection/20b58190948428aa77a583e526c08be96b94338704c4b9e276148a0b0b4fb2b6" "q-80647/selection/bc719e54be2c8060523f8e94ac84842d109a14e9f2239a7e02f7df837b08c2b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80647" :question/id #uuid "8290babb-692e-5ad7-9062-654c1e4847be" :question/math-academy-id "q-80647" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1, \\\\ {f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4) \\\\  & =a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1) \\\\  & =(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y}=(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x},$ respectively, we see that $a=-b.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{x}(1,1)=4+5e$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e \\\\ a(1)(1+(1))e+(1)e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ 2ae+e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ (2a+1)e & =5e \\\\ 2a+1 & =5 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb=-a=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80647/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80647"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80753/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "3693dd69-b57f-5c1f-bb68-959816b69ab2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80753/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "56e6d81f-21ef-5199-a42c-16f700341789" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80753/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "6a18faa3-dff2-5941-ba6d-4c6141863c00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80753/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "956f378e-ea70-5473-b6dd-1908244a5228" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80753/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "c36a3595-debd-59cf-8ba9-7cceb685bef7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80753/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6d6e27-c0b4-5426-a830-c42d01a5f2e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80753/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-80753/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-80753/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-80753/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-80753/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-80753/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80753" :question/id #uuid "7ba4f358-7a33-51dd-acb9-48ca0f438659" :question/math-academy-id "q-80753" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80753/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80753"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133250/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "58b984e8-8d85-56bd-a0bd-46baa8405516" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133250/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "771a2a03-d089-518a-a41f-aa6087b05277" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133250/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5ab858a5-08dc-57a0-b480-b087449f358b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133250/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f58e6147-e7da-500c-a26b-b1a024952a10" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133250/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1cfae88d-5964-5186-9b2d-13f952a86d89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133250/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec74724d-2c13-54b5-b763-e7902492b3ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-133250/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-133250/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-133250/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-133250/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-133250/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-133250/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133250" :question/id #uuid "3ec7f385-eb59-5b15-b685-39bfa2ef206b" :question/math-academy-id "q-133250" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-133250/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133250"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-36518"] :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=0$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+4y)=4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65127"] :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{3}y-x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x)={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y)={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}-2y)={e}^{x+y}-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}-2x)={e}^{x+y}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x-1})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-133331/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "3a55f028-8a02-5079-92d4-4667611c3bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133331/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "4edf0f67-a3b4-5a61-beb4-abbf38cecb7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133331/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "2370ec04-0d5e-5ddd-b69f-a42bcffb4665" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133331/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "73f77b02-25f6-5640-9a2d-ac7bac309a68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133331/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b0157d90-31ad-5d59-85a0-df00192683cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133331/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9620f6-7875-5312-9df0-897744b0a2e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-133331/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-133331/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-133331/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-133331/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-133331/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-133331/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133331" :question/id #uuid "3d9883d3-3319-557f-b21a-3239f1c7285f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133331" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{e}^{y+1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y+1}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=7x-3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-7y$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7x-3y)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-7y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-133331/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133331"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65130/selection/a1b2ccfba458a845fd8ee10e6daa31506496a50a50042e082232a7d8cf1de62a" :answer/id #uuid "3ca9636d-0ecb-566d-9621-1f42e1a97cb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65130/selection/b223011230e3168631e960160bc7cf09b40b4d9565d2d344a2f1fd9e90ab6950" :answer/id #uuid "dbc6de3b-527b-5686-9932-b083d4b7c861" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65130/selection/de65f68b9ebe8220ad685ae8fd317e4e85f600952fac99fcca3864668321a3a0" :answer/id #uuid "c907e5ae-fb7e-5134-b923-9214efbb261e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65130/selection/2a28a53216361474017108aa9cf0fffd0fb392524d53aeb00b78355e4fa703c6" :answer/id #uuid "7d52d356-d3ae-5c6f-85c6-fb3142bd4ca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65130/selection/30658d088d5b87ec1a64410993944d89cb17d534d10aadc650826eb087f134fe" :answer/id #uuid "d30507ea-78ce-507f-b59c-ce3770f4e604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65130/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd33d6fd-477a-5c19-bdfa-1c2d712e7246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-65130/selection/a1b2ccfba458a845fd8ee10e6daa31506496a50a50042e082232a7d8cf1de62a" :answer-field/choices ["q-65130/selection/a1b2ccfba458a845fd8ee10e6daa31506496a50a50042e082232a7d8cf1de62a" "q-65130/selection/b223011230e3168631e960160bc7cf09b40b4d9565d2d344a2f1fd9e90ab6950" "q-65130/selection/de65f68b9ebe8220ad685ae8fd317e4e85f600952fac99fcca3864668321a3a0" "q-65130/selection/2a28a53216361474017108aa9cf0fffd0fb392524d53aeb00b78355e4fa703c6" "q-65130/selection/30658d088d5b87ec1a64410993944d89cb17d534d10aadc650826eb087f134fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65130" :question/id #uuid "b61d9c66-e5e5-507e-a184-52505bf6709b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65130" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxx}(1,1)$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}(2+xy).$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy}(2+xy)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}(2+xy)+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}(3+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxx}(1,1)=(1)^{2}{e}^{1\\cdot 1}(3+(1)(1))=4e\\,\\,\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-65130/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65130"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65133/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1686b269-6051-5348-a71d-6ef3ecb6efe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65133/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "2764a86c-894f-54b7-86a7-f91277cd5125" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65133/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b2c21303-b93e-533d-98b4-47f943926a75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65133/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "2d994160-d5cf-5387-9263-a5c0183ffe32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65133/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "3308199c-8ebd-5baf-8e49-2a5040e1c8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65133/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "966f789b-7286-527b-8674-40261bb16e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-65133/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-65133/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-65133/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-65133/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-65133/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-65133/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65133" :question/id #uuid "5331f8c0-b4dd-5b70-8f83-8d7bfc1420c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-65133" :question/problem "Consider that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first, second and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ What is the value of ${f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xxy}(1,0)$ given that ${f}_{yx}(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "As the function and its first, second, and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ we must have ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)={f}_{xxy}(x,y).$ \n\nTherefore, we can swap the order of the subscripts in the first term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xxy}(1,0) & ={f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xyx}(1,0) \\\\  & ={f}_{yx}(1,0)+\\cancel{{f}_{xxy}(1,0)}-\\cancel{{f}_{xxy}(1,0)} \\\\  & ={f}_{yx}(1,0) \\\\  & =2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0)-(1)^{2}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot 0)+1 \\\\  & =2-0+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-65133/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65133"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133382/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "3416353f-2a29-5a6e-a8bd-6916d2fdf14b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133382/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "a6a6c7fd-ee6e-59aa-8050-418d17a8235f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133382/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "3fe0eb88-631a-52a5-b88b-286a16bbfcc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133382/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "424466a3-e251-5f36-b488-77274d4cad13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133382/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "784765c5-80df-596c-b0a0-95741fad0509" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133382/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5a3328-1405-5dcb-9210-3239cf85387d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-133382/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer-field/choices ["q-133382/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-133382/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-133382/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-133382/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-133382/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133382" :question/id #uuid "efe5e30e-cc66-5eb4-a8f0-4a3803fa0c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-133382" :question/problem "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{yz}\\cdot (\\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{yz}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot ({e}^{yz}+yz{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{yz}(1+yz)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}}{(1+1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =-\\frac{{e}^{1\\cdot 0}(1+1\\cdot 0)}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-133382/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133382"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133395/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "31d0f9ad-702c-5ff8-83fd-8d0489a89aa3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133395/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "d3eaec45-20f3-5890-9a36-3c2caab06b0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133395/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" :answer/id #uuid "2633835e-0ef5-5632-b8ad-14ed1fa13570" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133395/selection/a7643e7c9a6f47b7e23102a9f593aee2dc956fa29938bf5d470303f0587bc3b1" :answer/id #uuid "66295c51-1de0-5547-9cfe-2956ccc35aac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133395/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer/id #uuid "c19328cb-d0ac-5700-b492-1f64173fb90f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133395/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a456482-9bc2-5482-ac03-fbe52ddfbcdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-133395/selection/a7643e7c9a6f47b7e23102a9f593aee2dc956fa29938bf5d470303f0587bc3b1" :answer-field/choices ["q-133395/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-133395/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-133395/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" "q-133395/selection/a7643e7c9a6f47b7e23102a9f593aee2dc956fa29938bf5d470303f0587bc3b1" "q-133395/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133395" :question/id #uuid "3782e154-7f99-5423-a5fb-7beec602ccc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133395" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{yzx}(2,1,3)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{xy}(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y+z}}-\\frac{1}{x+y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(2,1,3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2-1+3}}-\\frac{1}{2+1+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-133395/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133395"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-133371/selection/c0299812b71a34ed2fa4c37dafae81d7f2eadb3cb2c295fdfc4a64b0c20eb53c" :answer/id #uuid "bd951c22-7641-5b5e-8504-2d807eb5ed42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-\\pi }+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133371/selection/d4cdb73eb4bce286f56c4b3e0a8b05862d4f6bb9d9eea3a1f57ba237919af600" :answer/id #uuid "201a8ec5-fde0-5565-9518-79e143cf8802" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133371/selection/fc148c0289c83713326dad63de2c742e550db1304399cab76ff128915cb185a1" :answer/id #uuid "1654d635-99fb-5dd0-9a08-cdc84ebe8e82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-{e}^{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133371/selection/4b613c0edc0b281b62da52c81e4f36ae66b590e39b135c9229118eb2e76ee730" :answer/id #uuid "639b398c-8d54-541c-81e5-b14d4bc840cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{1-\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133371/selection/0d5775caf8caf008b24a23219632040f0ab24811e4a6e7f201f09a3cebf242a5" :answer/id #uuid "4b03206f-42f9-55ab-9959-8ac0a5e1b262" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-133371/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee5328f6-9e3f-5182-afaf-b21b0795cf6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-133371/selection/0d5775caf8caf008b24a23219632040f0ab24811e4a6e7f201f09a3cebf242a5" :answer-field/choices ["q-133371/selection/c0299812b71a34ed2fa4c37dafae81d7f2eadb3cb2c295fdfc4a64b0c20eb53c" "q-133371/selection/d4cdb73eb4bce286f56c4b3e0a8b05862d4f6bb9d9eea3a1f57ba237919af600" "q-133371/selection/fc148c0289c83713326dad63de2c742e550db1304399cab76ff128915cb185a1" "q-133371/selection/4b613c0edc0b281b62da52c81e4f36ae66b590e39b135c9229118eb2e76ee730" "q-133371/selection/0d5775caf8caf008b24a23219632040f0ab24811e4a6e7f201f09a3cebf242a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-133371" :question/id #uuid "4d823fb6-9bd7-5485-9e31-831ff773dec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133371" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{xyx}(1,\\pi )$ if ${f}_{xx}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyx}(x,y) & ={f}_{xxy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{e}^{xy}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{xyx}(1,\\pi )={1}^{3}{e}^{1\\cdot \\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi ))=-{e}^{\\pi }\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-133371/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-133371"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3152"] :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b,$ if \n\n$$\nf_x(x,y) = 6xy+axy^3-5, \\qquad\nf_y(x,y) = 3x^2+bx^2y^2, \\qquad\nf_y(2,-1) = -24.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+ax{y}^{3}-5) \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+b{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. So, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yx}(x,y) \\\\ 6x+3ax{y}^{2} & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy^2,$ we see that $3a=2b.$ \n\nNow, since $f_y(2,-1)=-24,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-24 \\\\ 3(2)^{2}+b(2)^{2}(-1)^{2} & =-24 \\\\ 12+4b & =-24 \\\\ 4b & =-36 \\\\ b & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & =2b \\\\ 3a & =2(-9) \\\\ 3a & =-18 \\\\ a & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-6$ and $b=-9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3154"] :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=x+2y$ and $f_y(x,y)=2y-x$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$ and $f_y(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=-\\sin{x}\\sin{y}$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos{x}\\cos{y}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is,\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y).\n$$\n\nLet's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) \\neq f_{yx}(x,y),$ the first pair could *not* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) = f_{yx}(x,y),$ the second pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y),$  the third pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3153"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y,z).$ Given that all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z\\partial x} = -e^xy\\sin{(yz)},\n$$\n\n \n\nevaluate the following expression at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial z\\partial x\\partial z}+ \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f }{\\partial x \\partial z \\partial y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂z∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)-{e}^{x}yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)+yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\pi )^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=e{\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)+(\\pi )(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1))=e\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =e{\\pi }^{2}+e\\pi =e\\pi (\\pi +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
